1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,720
This is Recall, our solution to the RxAI Memory Retrieval Challenge, by Amaan Rais,

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:11,302
Dedar Alam and Frensi Qatipi. Recall finds the few turns that matter in months of

3
00:00:11,302 --> 00:00:15,024
chat history, and cites only what supports the answer.

4
00:00:15,724 --> 00:00:19,723
Here's the problem. Each test user has two hundred conversations:

5
00:00:19,723 --> 00:00:22,369
about one point six six million characters.

6
00:00:22,369 --> 00:00:25,692
Reactor takes sixteen thousand characters per message.

7
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:30,799
Some questions can't be answered at all, and near-miss conversations are planted to

8
00:00:30,799 --> 00:00:31,660
look relevant.

9
00:00:32,360 --> 00:00:35,867
So our code picks the evidence, and Reactor answers.

10
00:00:35,867 --> 00:00:41,464
We index every turn, route the question by type, retrieve the best turns with their

11
00:00:41,464 --> 00:00:46,724
dates, let Reactor answer from labelled excerpts, then check its citations, or

12
00:00:46,724 --> 00:00:47,264
abstain.

13
00:00:47,964 --> 00:00:52,942
A few decisions matter most. We search turns, not whole conversations, because a

14
00:00:52,942 --> 00:00:58,170
fact usually lives in one turn. Counting and ordering questions get a bigger budget.

15
00:00:58,170 --> 00:01:03,211
And dates are worked out in our code: every excerpt says how many days before the

16
00:01:03,211 --> 00:01:07,380
question it was said. All in plain Python, with no external models.

17
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:12,372
Citations follow the organisers' rule: they must support the answer.

18
00:01:12,372 --> 00:01:17,673
Reactor's own labels come first, then excerpts that actually contain its answer, and

19
00:01:17,673 --> 00:01:22,912
never the top result by default. When Reactor says the evidence is insufficient, we

20
00:01:22,912 --> 00:01:24,616
abstain, with no citations.

21
00:01:25,316 --> 00:01:28,543
This is our demo app. You chat with your own history.

22
00:01:28,543 --> 00:01:31,283
We ask: what did I bake for my niece's party?

23
00:01:31,283 --> 00:01:35,424
Recall searches forty-eight past conversations, and Reactor answers:

24
00:01:35,424 --> 00:01:39,260
lemon blueberry cake, from our chat on the twenty-sixth of May.

25
00:01:39,960 --> 00:01:42,798
Sources shows exactly what Reactor was given:

26
00:01:42,798 --> 00:01:47,654
a few excerpts, oldest first, each with its date and how many days before the

27
00:01:47,654 --> 00:01:52,637
question it was said. The newest is marked most recent, so a value that changed

28
00:01:52,637 --> 00:01:54,024
takes its latest form.

29
00:01:54,724 --> 00:01:59,117
The About page explains how it works, and its Results tab shows our measured

30
00:01:59,117 --> 00:02:01,660
numbers, straight from the evaluation files.

31
00:02:02,360 --> 00:02:07,383
That focus pays off. Our submitted run sends a median of eleven point three thousand

32
00:02:07,383 --> 00:02:12,407
characters per question: a hundred and forty-seven times less than the full history,

33
00:02:12,407 --> 00:02:16,952
and fifteen times less per user than loading the whole history into Reactor.

34
00:02:17,652 --> 00:02:22,736
By question type, a correct conversation is in our top five for over ninety-five

35
00:02:22,736 --> 00:02:27,757
percent of questions in every type. The hard part is multi-session and temporal

36
00:02:27,757 --> 00:02:31,380
questions, where the answer is spread over several turns.

37
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:36,755
Overall, a correct conversation is in the top five for ninety-eight point two

38
00:02:36,755 --> 00:02:41,309
percent of dev questions, and ninety-three point five percent on dev-large.

39
00:02:41,309 --> 00:02:46,349
On the test set, our submitted run answered a hundred and twenty-three of a hundred

40
00:02:46,349 --> 00:02:49,203
and forty-six questions, each with its sources.

41
00:02:49,203 --> 00:02:51,328
Accuracy comes from the organisers.

42
00:02:52,028 --> 00:02:56,858
Our submission is version three. Version two sharpens the evidence.

43
00:02:56,858 --> 00:03:02,770
Version three adds name questions: synonyms, and a boost for a name near the thing

44
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:08,754
asked about. And a lenient abstain line cut our test abstentions from twenty-one to

45
00:03:08,754 --> 00:03:09,908
sixteen percent.

46
00:03:10,608 --> 00:03:16,108
And we played by the rules: every answer comes from Reactor, one Reactor history per

47
00:03:16,108 --> 00:03:21,085
user, careful timeout handling, and evidence logs that make every prediction

48
00:03:21,085 --> 00:03:21,936
reproducible.

49
00:03:22,636 --> 00:03:27,307
Next: a local reranker for questions worded differently from the chat, dates

50
00:03:27,307 --> 00:03:32,224
computed in code, and checking that the lenient line doesn't let trick questions

51
00:03:32,224 --> 00:03:32,716
through.

52
00:03:33,416 --> 00:03:37,376
Thank you. That's Recall: the needle, not the haystack.

